{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE (Phase 1 complete, confirmed): the verdict scan is schema-blind, not the context assembly\n\nA `/rebar-debug` subagent completed Phase 1. **Root cause proven, and the leading hypothesis in\nHANDOFF-DF3A is confirmed as the TRIGGER but refuted as the CAUSE.**\n\n### The defect\n\nThe completion verifier's verdict is recovered by scanning the model's free text for JSON, and that\nscan selects the first **parseable** JSON object rather than the first that **satisfies the target\nschema**. When the verifier quotes a rebar `Dep` record in its prose *before* its verdict, that record\nis taken as the structured output and rejected — while a valid `CompletionVerdict` sits later in the\nsame reply.\n\nChain, with citations:\n\n1. `llm/capabilities.py:274-276` forces `native_structured_output: False` for all Claude models, so the\n   verifier always takes the prompted free-text branch — `llm/structured_run.py:201` builds `Agent(...)`\n   with **no `output_type`**. The verdict exists only as text; nothing provider-side enforces the schema.\n2. `llm/structured_run.py:213` -> `structured.parse_structured(str(result.output), model_cls)`.\n3. Selection is schema-blind in two places in `llm/structured.py`: `:138-140` `_FENCE_RE.search` takes\n   the **first** ```json fence in the reply; `:82-118`/`:146-150` `_first_json_object` returns the\n   **first balanced object that parses**, in document order (first-wins by design, from bug 67ee).\n   Neither consults `model_cls`. The `schema` argument reaches only json-repair (`:169-174`), the last\n   resort, never reached here.\n4. `llm/structured.py:206-209` raises; the bounded reask (`OUTPUT_RETRIES = 2`) re-pays the call and\n   re-raises as `step 'decide@then/verify' failed`.\n5. **Provenance of the bad payload is the `show_ticket` TOOL RESULT, not context assembly:**\n   `llm/pai_tools.py:219-232` returns the ticket as raw JSON including `deps`, whose shape is\n   `types.Dep` (`types.py:52-57`: `target_id`, `relation`, `link_uuid`) — exactly the reported payload.\n\n### Evidence\n\n* **Runtime capture** (one live run, monkeypatch on `parse_structured`): the verifier's actual reply for\n  b586 contains, in prose before the verdict,\n  ``- S2's deps include `{\"relation\": \"depends_on\", \"target_id\": \"f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31\"}` ✓`` and then a\n  fenced, **complete and valid** `CompletionVerdict` (PASS, 5 criteria). The key order — `relation`\n  first, target id last — matches pydantic's middle-truncated render in the original report exactly.\n* **Byte-for-byte reproduction**: a `Dep` record + a valid verdict through the real `parse_structured`\n  reproduces the reported error verbatim, including pydantic's truncation.\n* **Discriminating variants on the real captured reply** (nothing else changed): as emitted -> parses OK;\n  dep record in a ```json fence instead of single backticks -> **fails verbatim**; verdict emitted\n  unfenced, which the prompt directive at `structured.py:76-79` literally instructs (\"no prose, no\n  markdown fence\") -> **fails verbatim**. The model produced a valid verdict in every case; only the\n  *selection* differed.\n\n### What this corrects in this ticket's own premises\n\n* **Trigger confirmed:** the dep record appears only because re-parenting gave b586 children with a\n  `depends_on` link, and AC2 requires verifying that link — so the verifier calls `show_ticket`, reads\n  the JSON dep, and quotes it. It will recur on any parent whose children depend on each other.\n* **Cause refuted:** the links corrupt nothing. Reverting the re-parenting would fix nothing (and would\n  re-orphan two certified tickets).\n* **\"Model formatting drift\" refuted** — the model emitted a complete, schema-valid verdict.\n* **AC4's premise is refuted and needs re-scoping:** context assembly does **not** leak link records.\n  `llm/operations.py:58-61` renders deps as markdown (`- deps: depends_on->f2f3...`), never JSON. There\n  is nothing to fix at the assembly source; the JSON arrives via the tool result.\n* **Why it is intermittent:** the failure needs both a dep-quoting reply *and* an unlucky layout.\n\n### Fix shape (Phase 2 NOT entered)\n\nSchema-*conditional* refinement in `tolerant_parse`: prefer the first candidate that validates against\n`model_cls`; fall back to today's first-wins. ~15 lines, one site, one call site\n(`structured_run.py:213`). **This preserves every existing caller**, and the constraint is pinned:\n`tests/unit/test_structured.py:47-52` pins first-wins for two verdict objects but calls `tolerant_parse`\n**with no schema**, so a schema-conditional change is behaviour-identical there.\n`test_structured.py:126-158` encodes the neighbouring contract (recover a verdict past a preceding\nnon-JSON brace) — the variants above are that same contract violated by a preceding *parseable* object.\n\nPer HANDOFF-DF3A this is still NOT to be fixed by loosening `CompletionVerdict`. The fail-closed refusal\nis correct; the selection is the defect. Note the blast radius: `structured.py` is the shared parser\nevery LLM gate (plan-review, code-review, completion) depends on, so the repair needs the fix plan in\nthis description, a declared `file_impact`, and a `review-plan` attestation before it is written.\n\n### Prior art — this is the THIRD recurrence of this selection defect\n\nCited in-code at `structured.py:88-93` and `:186-200`: 67ee/messianic-wild-dassie, artsy-chain-hold,\ndash-lure-slag, slit-rubble-braid — the last described as blocking a completion-verifier close\nfail-closed. Same function, same class of bug.\n\n### Consequence for b586: it is CLOSED, honestly\n\nThe authorized reproduction run drew the lucky formatting and **passed**.\n[rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260] is now `closed` with a `completion-verifier` signature present — verified\nindependently by me (`status: closed`, `signature: completion-verifier`). No `--force-close` was used.\nThat is the certified close this epic held out for.\n\n*Not determined:* the exact byte layout of the two originally-failing replies (not captured). The\nvariants show both plausible layouts produce the error verbatim from the real reply, but the specific\none is inferred, not observed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785879298182707001,"uuid":"a57d2307-18da-4195-8bf4-d7a8720fa0ca"}